        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/144060585?v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/>\n\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Description</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">Documents that various ARIA-attributes are valid for the <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;svg&gt;</code> element.</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Motivation</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">It is not clear on this page if the <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;svg&gt;</code> element supports any other attributes than the ones listed. Most other pages have something along the lines of:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">This element's attributes include the <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Global_attributes\" rel=\"nofollow\">global attributes</a>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">(see e.g. <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/button#attributes\" rel=\"nofollow\"><code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;button&gt;</code></a>,  <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/div#attributes\" rel=\"nofollow\"><code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;div&gt;</code></a>)</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I saw some examples on MDN (e.g. on the <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Accessibility/ARIA/Reference/Attributes/aria-label\" rel=\"nofollow\"><code class=\"notranslate\">aria-label</code> page</a>) use ARIA labels on the <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;svg&gt;</code> element, but it's not clear on this page if this is supported or not. I had to go into the specification to find out.</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Additional details</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">The ARIA-attributes are listed as supported in the <a href=\"https://svgwg.org/svg2-draft/struct.html#SVGElement\" rel=\"nofollow\">specification</a>.</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Related issues and pull requests</h3>\n\n\n\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">None</p>",
